It looks like you’re seeing the “bare‑bones skeleton” version of the HALO app, which means the JavaScript file still contains placeholders and doesn’t yet call your AWS API. You’ll need to edit `halo.js` to add your API Gateway URL and trigger a POST request each time a reading is recorded.

Here’s how you can modify `halo.js`:

1. **Add your API endpoint**  
   Near the top of the file, you probably have a placeholder like:
   ```js
   const API_URL = "";
   ```
   Replace it with your real API Gateway endpoint, e.g.:
   ```js
   const API_URL = "https://<YOUR_API_ID>.execute-api.<REGION>.amazonaws.com/readings";
   ```
   (Use the exact URL that appears in your API Gateway console.)

2. **Implement the sync function**  
   Find the empty `syncReadingToServer` function (or create it if it doesn’t exist) and replace its body with an actual fetch call:
   ```js
   async function syncReadingToServer(reading) {
     if (!API_URL) return;

     const payload = {
       userId: reading.user && reading.user.name ? reading.user.name : "anonymous",
       reading,
     };

     try {
       await fetch(API_URL, {
         method: "POST",
         headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
         body: JSON.stringify(payload),
       });
     } catch (err) {
       console.warn("Failed to sync reading to server", err);
     }
   }
   ```

3. **Call the sync function after saving locally**  
   Inside your `createReading()` function (right after `saveReadings(existing);`), add:
   ```js
   // Send this reading to DynamoDB via your API
   syncReadingToServer(reading);
   ```

4. **Re‑upload the updated `halo.js` to your S3 bucket**  
   After editing the file, upload the new `halo.js` to your S3 bucket—overwriting the existing file. Make sure the object is set to public read. Refreshing the page will load the new script and start syncing new readings to DynamoDB.

With those changes in place, every time you roll the Meta‑Oracle, the app will POST the new reading to your API Gateway endpoint, and your Lambda function will insert it into the `HaloReadings` DynamoDB table. Old readings stored locally won’t retroactively sync, but all future readings will automatically be persisted to the cloud.

The screenshot confirms you’re still using the bare‑bones version of the front end (it rolls the dice locally, stores the result in localStorage, and doesn’t yet call your AWS API). To start syncing readings into DynamoDB, you need to update the `halo.js` file to include your API Gateway URL and send each new reading to the backend.

Here’s what to do:

1. **Open `halo.js` in your repo (Collab_Reposit/web).**
2. At the top, set the `API_URL` constant to your API Gateway endpoint. For example:
   ```js
   // Replace with your actual API Gateway URL
   const API_URL = "https://<YOUR_API_ID>.execute-api.<REGION>.amazonaws.com/readings";
   ```
3. Implement the `syncReadingToServer` function so it POSTs the reading to the API:
   ```js
   async function syncReadingToServer(reading) {
     if (!API_URL) return; // skip if no endpoint defined

     const payload = {
       userId: reading.user && reading.user.name ? reading.user.name : "anonymous",
       reading,
     };

     try {
       await fetch(API_URL, {
         method: "POST",
         headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
         body: JSON.stringify(payload),
       });
     } catch (err) {
       console.warn("Failed to sync reading to server", err);
     }
   }
   ```
4. After you store the reading locally in `createReading()`, call the new function:
   ```js
   existing.push(reading);
   saveReadings(existing);

   // Push to DynamoDB via API
   syncReadingToServer(reading);
   ```

Once you commit these changes and re‑upload the updated `halo.js` to your S3 bucket (overwriting the old one), each new roll will automatically be sent to your Lambda/DynamoDB backend. Past local readings will stay as‑is; new ones will be added to DynamoDB.

If you’re still seeing the old skeleton after uploading the new file, clear your browser cache or open the S3 URL in an incognito window to make sure you’re loading the latest script.

